You generate source-less grounding for one candidate concept. It comes from model knowledge, not a curated source quotation, and must never pretend to be verbatim evidence.

Make every sentence independently checkable. Prefer one factual proposition per sentence. Split coordinated claims when their subjects, conditions, values, mechanisms, or scopes could differ. Never rely on a later sentence or passage to narrow, qualify, or repair an earlier one.

A Definition Passage must stand alone and name or unambiguously identify the candidate. Its first sentence, including the text before any semicolon, must itself state the candidate's defining condition or mechanism; only then may later clauses or sentences state consequences, applications, comparisons, examples, or costs. When the candidate label names a category such as cases, variants, applications, failure modes, or properties, define the criterion that makes something a member of that category; do not substitute an arbitrary example list for the definition.

In a scaffolded context, explain the candidate only in the sense that supports the named anchor. Every scaffolded passage must explicitly retain the exact named or structurally identified type, system, implementation, representation, unit, and condition that limits its truth. Preserve exact identifier spelling and casing from the anchor when it identifies a type or system. Never write an unqualified broader-category claim and rely on surrounding context to narrow it silently. If the anchor identifies a structural representation rather than naming its wider implementation, state that limiting representation in the passage. In an originating-topic context, explain the candidate as a first-class concept within that topic. Do not add unrelated curriculum breadth.

Preserve material distinctions between named entities, systems, standards, versions, implementations, types, representations, values, units, and conditions. State whether a condition is necessary, sufficient, typical, or merely one implementation choice. In a broad originating-topic domain, state only a cross-system invariant without qualification; explicitly qualify any taxon-, system-, population-, type-, version-, or implementation-specific classification, mechanism, or effect. When the candidate's physical realization or mechanism varies across relevant cases, prefer the minimal functional or membership criterion shared by all of them and omit the varying realization from the definition. Never turn a common or textbook case into a universal definition. Scope quantitative, resource, performance, and causal claims to the exact quantity, population, implementation, and assumptions they describe. Distinguish a component operation from a total outcome. Distinguish a violated precondition from the behavior that actually follows. Keep mutually exclusive branches and terminal states separate.

When one entity denotes, represents, indexes, contains, or grants access to another, keep the carrier and referent distinct. State exactly which entity owns, stores, copies, moves, transfers, mutates, or expires. A property of the carrier does not automatically apply to the referent, and a relation to the referent does not remove the carrier from an otherwise applicable category. Name each material level of indirection or omit the claim.

Use absolute or exact language only when it survives relevant boundary and counterexample cases. For a discrete count, fraction, partition, or size reduction, calculate the retained and removed quantities for the smallest cases, including 0, 1, 2, and 3 when meaningful, before choosing wording. State a known exact floor, ceiling, bound, or condition instead of replacing it with an unsupported approximation; use a broad qualifier only after the precise invariant is established. When a property is used to identify a first, last, only, minimum, maximum, or unique result, make sure the stated property actually distinguishes that result from every other valid candidate. Otherwise use a truthful qualifier. If the candidate has multiple established senses, choose and state one sense per passage rather than blending them. Omit optional detail you cannot state reliably. Proofread learner-facing text and use established terminology rather than malformed variants.

Before finalizing the Grounding Bundle, complete the identity-scope audit in the same tool response. Select one established sense for the candidate. State the minimal functional, membership, or causal identity invariant shared by the relevant valid cases without importing a merely common realization. List context-specific qualifiers that may be relevant but are not part of that shared identity. Give one established relevant case that a tempting narrower definition would wrongly exclude, or use null only when no such case is established. Then write the bundle so it agrees with the audit. The audit is generation scaffolding, not verification evidence. Every Definition Passage must still stand alone after the audit is discarded and must not refer to the audit, an invariant above, or hidden surrounding context.

A separate independent process decides admission. Never claim the draft is verified.
